window.MCQ = [{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Name two AWS services where you can store secrets.</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"S3 and EBS","ok":false},{"t":"Secrets Manager and SSM Parameter Store","ok":true},{"t":"IAM and CloudTrail","ok":false},{"t":"ECR and ECS","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Secrets Manager adds automatic rotation (paid). Parameter Store SecureString is free on the standard tier.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m0--type-html-html-name-two-aws-s"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How do Regions and Availability Zones relate?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"An AZ contains several Regions","ok":false},{"t":"A Region is a geographic area containing 3+ isolated AZs (separate data centres) linked by fast private networking","ok":true},{"t":"They're the same thing","ok":false},{"t":"AZs are only for backups","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Spread across <strong>2+ AZs</strong> for high availability. Memos uses eu-west-1a and 1b.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m1--type-html-html-how-do-regions"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is auto scaling?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Manually resizing servers","ok":false},{"t":"Automatically adding capacity when load rises and removing it when load falls, while replacing unhealthy instances","ok":true},{"t":"Moving apps between Regions","ok":false},{"t":"Making disks bigger","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Policies: target tracking, step, scheduled and predictive.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m2--type-html-html-what-is-auto-s"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which services help protect against a <strong>DDoS</strong> attack?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"IAM and Organizations","ok":false},{"t":"AWS Shield, WAF, CloudFront and Route 53 (plus ALB and auto scaling to absorb load)","ok":true},{"t":"S3 and Glacier","ok":false},{"t":"CloudTrail only","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Shield Standard is free and automatic. Shield Advanced adds DDoS response support and cost protection.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m3--type-html-html-which-services"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is an AMI?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"An IAM policy type","ok":false},{"t":"An Amazon Machine Image: the template (OS and software) an EC2 instance launches from","ok":true},{"t":"A monitoring agent","ok":false},{"t":"A load balancer type","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Golden AMIs can be built with <strong>Packer</strong>.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m4--type-html-html-what-is-an-ami"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which network object lets a server in a VPC <strong>receive</strong> traffic from the internet?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"NAT gateway","ok":false},{"t":"Internet gateway (with a public IP and a route to it)","ok":true},{"t":"VPC endpoint","ok":false},{"t":"Transit Gateway","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>NAT is <strong>outbound-only</strong>. Inbound needs an IGW route, a public IP (or a public load balancer) and security group rules.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m5--type-html-html-which-network-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In a VPC with public and private subnets, where should database servers go?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Public subnets, for easy access","ok":false},{"t":"Private subnets, reachable only from the app tier's security group","ok":true},{"t":"Any subnet, as long as the password is strong","ok":false},{"t":"In the default VPC","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Databases should never be directly reachable from the internet.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m6--type-html-html-in-a-vpc-with-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which is an EC2 security best practice?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Open port 22 to 0.0.0.0/0 for convenience","ok":false},{"t":"Use least-privilege IAM roles, tight security groups, SSM Session Manager instead of open SSH, and keep the OS patched","ok":true},{"t":"Share one key pair with the whole team","ok":false},{"t":"Put access keys in user data","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Instance <strong>roles</strong> give temporary credentials, so you never need keys on the box.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m7--type-html-html-which-is-an-ec"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Traffic peaks every Wednesday and Friday, 9 AM to 7 PM. What's the best scaling approach?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Scale on 100% CPU","ok":false},{"t":"Scheduled scaling to add capacity before 9 AM on those days, with target tracking for anything unexpected","ok":true},{"t":"Buy the biggest instance permanently","ok":false},{"t":"Disable auto scaling","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Predictable patterns → schedule them, and react to the rest.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m8--type-html-html-traffic-peaks-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You want to reduce load on an EC2 instance <strong>as soon as</strong> CPU hits 100%. What's the better answer?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Add a CloudWatch alarm at 100% that adds instances","ok":false},{"t":"Scale at a lower target (60–70%) so new capacity is ready before you hit 100%","ok":true},{"t":"Reboot the instance at 100%","ok":false},{"t":"Switch to a smaller instance","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>By the time CPU is at 100%, users are already suffering, and new instances take minutes to arrive.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m9--type-html-html-you-want-to-re"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Name managed Lambda runtimes.</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Node.js, Python, Java, .NET and Ruby (Go and Rust run on the OS-only <code>provided.al2023</code> runtime)","ok":true},{"t":"Only Python","ok":false},{"t":"PHP and Perl only","ok":false},{"t":"Lambda only runs containers","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>You can also package Lambda as a container image.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m10--type-html-html-name-managed-l"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What makes a subnet <strong>public</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A checkbox in the console","ok":false},{"t":"Its route table sends 0.0.0.0/0 to an internet gateway","ok":true},{"t":"Having more than 256 IPs","ok":false},{"t":"Being in AZ a","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Private subnets route 0.0.0.0/0 to a NAT gateway, or have no default route at all.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Revision notes","level":"Beginner","id":"m11--type-html-html-what-makes-a-s"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Look at the diagram. What will happen to traffic from the internet trying to reach the ECS task directly?</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  I((Internet)) --> IGW[IGW] --> ALB[\"ALB · public subnet\"]\n  ALB -->|8081 from ALB SG| T[\"ECS task · private subnet<br/>no public IP\"]\n  T -->|outbound| NAT[NAT gateway] --> IGW"}],"options":[{"t":"It reaches the task through the NAT gateway","ok":false},{"t":"It can't: the task has no public IP, and NAT only allows outbound-started connections","ok":true},{"t":"It reaches the task on port 8081","ok":false},{"t":"It's redirected to HTTPS","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The only way in is through the ALB, and the ECS security group only accepts 8081 from the ALB's security group.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Revision notes","level":"Intermediate","id":"m12--type-html-html-look-at-the-di"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What do <strong>RPO</strong> and <strong>RTO</strong> mean?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Region Placement Option / Route Table Owner","ok":false},{"t":"RPO = the most data you can afford to lose (in time); RTO = the most downtime you can afford","ok":true},{"t":"Both are measured in GB","ok":false},{"t":"RPO is for networks, RTO for storage","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Lower RPO/RTO usually costs more: backup &amp; restore → pilot light → warm standby → active/active.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Revision notes","level":"Beginner","id":"m13--type-html-html-what-do-rpo-an"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Under the shared responsibility model, who patches the OS of an EC2 instance, and of a Fargate task's host?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"AWS for both","ok":false},{"t":"You for both","ok":false},{"t":"You patch EC2; AWS patches Fargate's hosts","ok":true},{"t":"Nobody, because patching is automatic","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The more managed the service, the more AWS takes on. You still own your container image's contents.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Revision notes","level":"Beginner","id":"m14--type-html-html-under-the-shar"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which is the AWS <strong>audit log</strong> of API calls: who did what, and when?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"CloudWatch Metrics","ok":false},{"t":"CloudTrail","ok":true},{"t":"AWS Config","ok":false},{"t":"X-Ray","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Config records <strong>resource configuration history</strong>. CloudTrail records <strong>API calls</strong>.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Revision notes","level":"Beginner","id":"m15--type-html-html-which-is-the-a"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>S3 durability vs availability?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"They're the same","ok":false},{"t":"Durability = how unlikely data is to be lost (eleven nines); availability = how often you can reach it (99.99% for Standard)","ok":true},{"t":"Durability is about speed","ok":false},{"t":"Availability is about encryption","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Data is almost never lost, but there can still be brief moments when you can't reach it.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Revision notes","level":"Intermediate","id":"m16--type-html-html-s3-durability-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is a VPC?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A virtual private computer","ok":false},{"t":"Your own isolated network in AWS where you choose the IP range, subnets, routing and firewalls","ok":true},{"t":"A VPN to your office","ok":false},{"t":"A type of EC2 instance","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Every Region also has a \"default VPC\" for convenience, but real workloads should use their own.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m17--type-html-html-what-is-a-vpc-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Describe a three-tier architecture on AWS.</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Three EC2 instances in one public subnet","ok":false},{"t":"Web tier (ALB in public subnets) → app tier (EC2/ECS in private subnets, allowed only from the ALB) → data tier (RDS Multi-AZ in isolated subnets, allowed only from the app tier)","ok":true},{"t":"Three Regions with one server each","ok":false},{"t":"Lambda, S3 and CloudFront only","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Memos has the first two tiers. Its data tier is missing because SQLite lives inside the task.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m18--type-html-html-describe-a-thr"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which load balancer types does AWS offer?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Only ALB","ok":false},{"t":"ALB (L7 HTTP), NLB (L4 TCP/UDP, static IPs), Gateway LB (L3 appliances) and the legacy Classic","ok":true},{"t":"ALB and CloudFront","ok":false},{"t":"Route 53 and NLB","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Memos uses an ALB.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Revision notes","level":"Beginner","id":"m19--type-html-html-which-load-bal"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What problem does CI/CD solve?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It removes the need for testing","ok":false},{"t":"Manual, infrequent, error-prone builds and releases: it automates building, testing and deploying small changes often","ok":true},{"t":"It replaces version control","ok":false},{"t":"It makes code run faster","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Small, frequent, automated releases are safer than big, rare, manual ones.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m20--type-html-html-what-problem-d"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is continuous integration?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Developers merge small changes to the main branch often, and every change is automatically built and tested","ok":true},{"t":"Deploying to production every hour","ok":false},{"t":"Running tests once a week","ok":false},{"t":"Integrating two companies' systems","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>CI catches integration problems within minutes of a push.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m21--type-html-html-what-is-contin"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Continuous delivery vs continuous deployment?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"They're the same","ok":false},{"t":"Delivery: every change is releasable, but a human approves production; deployment: changes go to production automatically","ok":true},{"t":"Deployment is only for infrastructure","ok":false},{"t":"Delivery skips tests","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Memos does continuous <strong>deployment</strong>: build → deploy → health check, with no approval step.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m22--type-html-html-continuous-del"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is a build <strong>artefact</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A broken build","ok":false},{"t":"The versioned output of a build (a binary, package or container image) that gets deployed","ok":true},{"t":"The source code","ok":false},{"t":"A test report only","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Memos' artefact is the <code>memos:&lt;git-sha&gt;</code> image in ECR.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m23--type-html-html-what-is-a-buil"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why is automated testing important in CI/CD?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It makes builds slower","ok":false},{"t":"It gives fast, consistent feedback and stops broken code reaching production without relying on someone remembering to test","ok":true},{"t":"It replaces code review","ok":false},{"t":"It's required by GitHub","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>No tests means CI/CD just ships bugs faster.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m24--type-html-html-why-is-automat"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What should happen when a pipeline stage fails?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Skip that stage and continue","ok":false},{"t":"Stop the pipeline, alert the team, and fix the cause before anything moves on","ok":true},{"t":"Deploy anyway and fix in production","ok":false},{"t":"Delete the pipeline","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A failed stage is the safety net working.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m25--type-html-html-what-should-ha"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is a <strong>blue/green</strong> deployment?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Deploying to two Regions at once","ok":false},{"t":"Running the new version (green) alongside the old (blue), then switching traffic over all at once, with instant switch-back","ok":true},{"t":"Gradually replacing instances one by one","ok":false},{"t":"Testing in a green-themed environment","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Costs double capacity during the switch, but rollback is a single traffic flip.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m26--type-html-html-what-is-a-blue"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is a <strong>canary</strong> deployment?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Releasing to a small percentage of users first, watching the metrics, then widening","ok":true},{"t":"Deploying only on Fridays","ok":false},{"t":"Replacing everything at once","ok":false},{"t":"A deployment that tests birds","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Weighted target groups or Route 53 weighted records can do canaries on AWS.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m27--type-html-html-what-is-a-cana"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is a <strong>rolling</strong> deployment?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Rebuilding the image each time","ok":false},{"t":"Replacing old instances or tasks with new ones gradually, so some capacity is always serving","ok":true},{"t":"Deploying to all servers at the same instant","ok":false},{"t":"Rolling back automatically","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>ECS services do rolling deployments by default, which is how Memos updates.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m28--type-html-html-what-is-a-roll"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why have separate dev, staging and prod environments?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"To triple the cost","ok":false},{"t":"To test changes safely in production-like conditions before real users see them","ok":true},{"t":"Because Terraform requires three","ok":false},{"t":"So each developer has a production","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Promote the <strong>same artefact</strong> through each environment.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m29--type-html-html-why-have-separ"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Many developers merge often and <code>main</code> keeps breaking. What helps most?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Let one person merge everything","ok":false},{"t":"Required PR checks (build + tests), branch protection, small PRs and fast feedback, plus feature flags for unfinished work","ok":true},{"t":"Merge once a month","ok":false},{"t":"Turn off CI to go faster","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Make it <strong>impossible</strong> to merge red, and keep changes small.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m30--type-html-html-many-developer"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>The pipeline passes, but the app fails in staging. What are the likely causes?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The tests are too strict","ok":false},{"t":"Environment differences: config or variables, secrets, IAM permissions, networking, database schema or versions that the tests didn't cover","ok":true},{"t":"The Git history is corrupted","ok":false},{"t":"The CI runner is too slow","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Add integration and smoke tests against staging, and keep environments consistent with IaC.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m31--type-html-html-the-pipeline-p"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which line lets a GitHub Actions job request an OIDC token for AWS?</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"yaml","code":"jobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    permissions:\n      id-token: ???\n      contents: read"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>id-token: read</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>id-token: write</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>id-token: none</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>id-token: admin</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>write</code> lets the job <strong>request</strong> an ID token. Without it, <code>configure-aws-credentials</code> can't do OIDC.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"Revision notes","level":"Intermediate","id":"m32--type-html-html-which-line-let"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why pin third-party GitHub Actions to a full commit SHA rather than a tag like <code>@v4</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"SHAs run faster","ok":false},{"t":"Tags can be moved to point at different (possibly malicious) code; a SHA can't change","ok":true},{"t":"GitHub requires it","ok":false},{"t":"Tags don't work for private repos","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Your workflow runs with your credentials, so supply-chain safety matters.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"Revision notes","level":"Intermediate","id":"m33--type-html-html-why-pin-third-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which trigger lets you run a workflow manually, with inputs, from the GitHub UI?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>push</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>schedule</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>workflow_dispatch</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>pull_request_target</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Memos' <code>deploy.yml</code> uses it with an <code>image_tag</code> input for rollbacks, and <code>destroy.yml</code> is dispatch-only.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"Revision notes","level":"Beginner","id":"m34--type-html-html-which-trigger-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is a container?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A small virtual machine with its own kernel","ok":false},{"t":"An isolated Linux process (namespaces + cgroups) packaged with its app and dependencies","ok":true},{"t":"A zip file","ok":false},{"t":"A type of server","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>It shares the host kernel. That's why it starts in seconds.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m35--type-html-html-what-is-a-cont"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is a Dockerfile used for?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Defining, step by step, how to build an image","ok":true},{"t":"Running several containers together","ok":false},{"t":"Storing container logs","ok":false},{"t":"Configuring Kubernetes","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Running several containers together is Docker <strong>Compose</strong>'s job.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m36--type-html-html-what-is-a-dock"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command opens a shell inside a running container called <code>memos</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>docker run memos sh</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>docker exec -it memos sh</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>docker attach -shell memos</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>docker ssh memos</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>run</code> starts a <strong>new</strong> container. <code>exec</code> runs a command in an <strong>existing</strong> one.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m37--type-html-html-which-command-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is a <strong>dangling</strong> image?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"An image with a security issue","ok":false},{"t":"An image layer with no tag (shown as <code>&lt;none&gt;</code>), usually left over after rebuilding a tag","ok":true},{"t":"An image being pushed","ok":false},{"t":"An image over 1 GB","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Clean up with <code>docker image prune</code>.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m38--type-html-html-what-is-a-dang"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>COPY</code> vs <code>ADD</code> in a Dockerfile?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"COPY just copies files; ADD can also extract local tar files and download URLs, so prefer COPY unless you need that","ok":true},{"t":"ADD is faster","ok":false},{"t":"COPY only works with folders","ok":false},{"t":"They're identical","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Being explicit (COPY) avoids surprises.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m39--type-html-html-copy-vs-add-in"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>EXPOSE 8081</code> in a Dockerfile vs <code>-p 8081:8081</code> at run time?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Both publish the port","ok":false},{"t":"EXPOSE only documents the port; <code>-p</code> actually publishes it to the host","ok":true},{"t":"EXPOSE opens a firewall rule","ok":false},{"t":"<code>-p</code> is only for Kubernetes","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Without <code>-p</code> (or a service or orchestrator mapping it), nothing outside can reach the port.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m40--type-html-html-expose-8081-in"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>RUN</code>, <code>CMD</code> and <code>ENTRYPOINT</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"All three run at build time","ok":false},{"t":"RUN runs at build time; ENTRYPOINT is the main executable at start-up; CMD gives default arguments that can be overridden","ok":true},{"t":"CMD runs at build time","ok":false},{"t":"ENTRYPOINT only works with shell scripts","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Memos uses <code>ENTRYPOINT [\"memos\"]</code>.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m41--type-html-html-run-cmd-and-en"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>If you set a 512 MB memory <strong>limit</strong> on a container, is that memory reserved for it?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Yes, the host sets aside 512 MB immediately","ok":false},{"t":"No, it's a maximum; the container is killed (OOM, exit 137) if it tries to go over","ok":true},{"t":"It's a minimum guarantee","ok":false},{"t":"Limits are ignored by Docker","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A <strong>reservation</strong> (or a request in Kubernetes) is the guarantee. A <strong>limit</strong> is the cap.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m42--type-html-html-if-you-set-a-5"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Is it a good idea to run a <strong>stateful</strong> app (like a database) in a container?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Never","ok":false},{"t":"It can be done, but only with persistent volumes, backups and care; managed databases are often simpler","ok":true},{"t":"Always: containers are ideal for state","ok":false},{"t":"Only if it runs as root","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Containers are disposable. Memos' SQLite-in-the-container problem is exactly this lesson.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m43--type-html-html-is-it-a-good-i"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is Docker Compose for?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Defining and running multiple related containers (app + database) from one YAML file","ok":true},{"t":"Building images faster","ok":false},{"t":"Production orchestration across many hosts","ok":false},{"t":"Scanning images","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>For production across many hosts you'd use ECS or Kubernetes. Compose is great for local development.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m44--type-html-html-what-is-docker"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is an <strong>orphaned</strong> (dangling) volume, and how do you remove it?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A volume shared by two containers; <code>docker rm -v</code>","ok":false},{"t":"A volume no container uses any more; <code>docker volume prune</code>","ok":true},{"t":"A volume that's full; <code>docker volume grow</code>","ok":false},{"t":"A volume with root files; <code>chmod</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Removing containers doesn't remove their named volumes. They pile up until pruned.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m45--type-html-html-what-is-an-orp"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Name a real limitation of containers compared with VMs.</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"They're slower to start","ok":false},{"t":"Weaker isolation, because they share the host kernel, and they can't run a different OS kernel","ok":true},{"t":"They use more RAM","ok":false},{"t":"They can't run Linux","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>For strong multi-tenant isolation people use VMs, or micro-VMs such as Firecracker (which Fargate uses underneath).</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m46--type-html-html-name-a-real-li"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which is a production best practice for containers?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Run as root for simplicity","ok":false},{"t":"Use the <code>latest</code> tag everywhere","ok":false},{"t":"Use small trusted base images, run as non-root, inject secrets at runtime and pin image versions","ok":true},{"t":"Store data inside the container","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Memos follows these: Alpine, user 1001, SHA tags. Secrets would come from Secrets Manager.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m47--type-html-html-which-is-a-pro"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Look at the Dockerfile. What will be in the <strong>final</strong> image?</p>"},{"type":"code","lang":"dockerfile","code":"FROM golang:1.25-alpine AS backend\nCOPY . .\nRUN go build -o /memos ./cmd/memos\n\nFROM alpine:3.21\nCOPY --from=backend /memos /usr/local/bin/memos\nENTRYPOINT [\"memos\"]"}],"options":[{"t":"Go, the source code and the binary","ok":false},{"t":"Only Alpine plus the <code>memos</code> binary","ok":true},{"t":"Just the source code","ok":false},{"t":"Both stages stacked","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The final image is <strong>only the last stage</strong>. The Go toolchain and source stay behind in the builder.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m48--type-html-html-look-at-the-do"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is Git?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A cloud hosting service","ok":false},{"t":"A distributed version control system: every clone has the full history","ok":true},{"t":"A programming language","ok":false},{"t":"A CI/CD tool","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>GitHub, GitLab and Bitbucket <strong>host</strong> Git repositories. Git itself is the tool.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m49--type-html-html-what-is-git-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How is Git different from SVN?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Git can't do branches","ok":false},{"t":"Git is distributed (full history locally, works offline); SVN is centralised (one server holds the history)","ok":true},{"t":"SVN is newer","ok":false},{"t":"They are the same","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Distributed also makes branching and merging much cheaper in Git.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m50--type-html-html-how-is-git-dif"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>git fetch</code> vs <code>git pull</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"fetch downloads new commits without changing your files; pull fetches <strong>and</strong> merges (or rebases) into your branch","ok":true},{"t":"They're identical","ok":false},{"t":"pull only works on main","ok":false},{"t":"fetch deletes local changes","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Use fetch to look before you merge.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m51--type-html-html-git-fetch-vs-g"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does <code>git cherry-pick &lt;sha&gt;</code> do?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Deletes a commit","ok":false},{"t":"Applies one specific commit from another branch onto your current branch","ok":true},{"t":"Merges a whole branch","ok":false},{"t":"Picks the best branch automatically","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Useful for bringing a single bug fix across without merging everything.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m52--type-html-html-what-does-git-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is <code>HEAD</code> in Git?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The first commit","ok":false},{"t":"A pointer to the commit you currently have checked out (usually the tip of your branch)","ok":true},{"t":"The remote server","ok":false},{"t":"The newest tag","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>\"Detached HEAD\" means you checked out a commit directly, not a branch.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m53--type-html-html-what-is-head-i"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>When do you use <code>git stash</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"To set aside uncommitted changes temporarily, e.g. to switch branches","ok":true},{"t":"To delete a branch","ok":false},{"t":"To push to a remote","ok":false},{"t":"To undo a pushed commit","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>git stash pop</code> restores the changes and drops the stash. <code>git stash apply</code> restores them and keeps it.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m54--type-html-html-when-do-you-us"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Fork vs clone vs branch?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"They're all the same","ok":false},{"t":"A fork is your own server-side copy of someone's repo; a clone is a local copy of a repo; a branch is a line of work inside a repo","ok":true},{"t":"A branch is a copy on GitHub","ok":false},{"t":"A clone only copies the latest commit","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Open-source flow: <strong>fork</strong> → <strong>clone</strong> your fork → <strong>branch</strong> → PR back.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m55--type-html-html-fork-vs-clone-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You want to throw away your last 2 <strong>local, unpushed</strong> commits and their changes. Which command?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>git revert HEAD~2</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>git reset --hard HEAD~2</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>git stash</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>git fetch --prune</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>reset --hard</code> moves the branch back and discards changes. Only do it on unshared work. Use <code>revert</code> for pushed commits.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m56--type-html-html-you-want-to-th"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command shows the differences between two commits?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>git log a b</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>git diff &lt;sha1&gt; &lt;sha2&gt;</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>git status a b</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>git compare a b</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>git diff main..feature</code> compares two branches.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m57--type-html-html-which-command-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How can you undo a rebase that went wrong?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It can't be undone","ok":false},{"t":"Find the pre-rebase commit in <code>git reflog</code> and <code>git reset --hard</code> to it","ok":true},{"t":"<code>git rebase --undo</code>","ok":false},{"t":"Delete <code>.git</code> and clone again","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The <strong>reflog</strong> records where HEAD has been, which is your safety net for almost any local mistake.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m58--type-html-html-how-can-you-un"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How do you edit a commit <strong>further back</strong> than the last one?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>git commit --amend</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>git rebase -i &lt;sha&gt;^</code>, mark it <code>edit</code> (or <code>fixup</code>), make the change, then continue","ok":true},{"t":"<code>git revert</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>git cherry-pick</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>--amend</code> only edits the <strong>latest</strong> commit. Interactive rebase rewrites older ones. Only do it on unpushed or your own branches.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m59--type-html-html-how-do-you-edi"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is Kubernetes?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A container image format","ok":false},{"t":"An open-source container orchestration platform that deploys, scales, heals and networks containers across many machines","ok":true},{"t":"A Linux distribution","ok":false},{"t":"AWS's container service","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>It started at Google and is now run by the CNCF. EKS is AWS's managed Kubernetes.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m60--type-html-html-what-is-kubern"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What problems does Kubernetes solve?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Writing application code","ok":false},{"t":"Scheduling containers onto machines, self-healing, scaling, service discovery and load balancing, and rolling updates","ok":true},{"t":"Building images","ok":false},{"t":"Version control","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>All of it comes from the declarative desired state plus control loops.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m61--type-html-html-what-problems-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Docker vs Kubernetes?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Kubernetes replaces images","ok":false},{"t":"Docker builds and runs containers on one host; Kubernetes orchestrates containers across a cluster of hosts","ok":true},{"t":"They are competitors doing the same thing","ok":false},{"t":"Docker is only for Windows","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Kubernetes runs container images using a runtime such as containerd.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m62--type-html-html-docker-vs-kube"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is a <strong>Pod</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The smallest deployable unit: one or more containers sharing a network namespace (one IP) and volumes","ok":true},{"t":"A physical server","ok":false},{"t":"A cluster of nodes","ok":false},{"t":"A Docker image","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Containers in the same pod talk over <code>localhost</code>.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m63--type-html-html-what-is-a-pod-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How do containers in the same Pod communicate?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Through the Ingress","ok":false},{"t":"Over <code>localhost</code>, because they share a network namespace, and through shared volumes","ok":true},{"t":"Only via a Service","ok":false},{"t":"They can't","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A common pattern is a sidecar (e.g. a log shipper) next to the app container.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m64--type-html-html-how-do-contain"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does a <strong>Deployment</strong> do?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Builds images","ok":false},{"t":"Manages ReplicaSets to keep N pods running and performs rolling updates and rollbacks","ok":true},{"t":"Exposes pods to the internet","ok":false},{"t":"Stores secrets","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/memos</code> rolls back.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m65--type-html-html-what-does-a-de"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>When do you use a <strong>StatefulSet</strong> instead of a Deployment?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"For stateless web apps","ok":false},{"t":"For stateful apps that need stable network identities and their own persistent volume each (databases, Kafka)","ok":true},{"t":"For batch jobs","ok":false},{"t":"For DaemonSets","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Pods get stable names (<code>db-0</code>, <code>db-1</code>) and keep their own storage across restarts.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m66--type-html-html-when-do-you-us"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How do you restrict which pods can talk to each other?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Security groups only","ok":false},{"t":"NetworkPolicies (enforced by a CNI plugin that supports them, such as Calico or Cilium)","ok":true},{"t":"Namespaces alone block traffic","ok":false},{"t":"ConfigMaps","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>By default every pod can reach every pod. NetworkPolicies are how you lock that down.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m67--type-html-html-how-do-you-res"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is the role of <strong>etcd</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Running containers","ok":false},{"t":"A consistent key-value store holding all cluster state and configuration","ok":true},{"t":"Load balancing traffic","ok":false},{"t":"Building images","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Back it up. Losing etcd means losing the cluster's state.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m68--type-html-html-what-is-the-ro"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is the <strong>kube-apiserver</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The control plane's front door: every request (kubectl, controllers, kubelets) goes through it, and it validates and stores changes in etcd","ok":true},{"t":"The node agent","ok":false},{"t":"The DNS server","ok":false},{"t":"The container runtime","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Nothing talks to etcd directly. Everything goes through the API server.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m69--type-html-html-what-is-the-ku"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is an <strong>Ingress controller</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A firewall for nodes","ok":false},{"t":"A component that implements Ingress rules: an HTTP(S) proxy routing outside traffic to Services by host or path (e.g. NGINX, or the AWS Load Balancer Controller → ALB)","ok":true},{"t":"A type of Pod security policy","ok":false},{"t":"The scheduler","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>An Ingress object does nothing without a controller.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m70--type-html-html-what-is-an-ing"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What happens if a control-plane (master) node fails in a single-master cluster?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"All pods are deleted immediately","ok":false},{"t":"Running pods keep running, but you can't make changes, and failed pods aren't rescheduled until the control plane is back","ok":true},{"t":"Nothing at all","ok":false},{"t":"Worker nodes become masters","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>That's why production uses <strong>3+ control-plane nodes</strong> (or a managed one, such as EKS).</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m71--type-html-html-what-happens-i"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How do you build a highly available Kubernetes cluster?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"One big node","ok":false},{"t":"Several control-plane nodes with etcd quorum, worker nodes spread across AZs, multiple replicas per app, plus PodDisruptionBudgets","ok":true},{"t":"Run everything in one namespace","ok":false},{"t":"Disable the scheduler","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>EKS runs the control plane across 3 AZs for you.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m72--type-html-html-how-do-you-bui"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What are <strong>taints and tolerations</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Image vulnerabilities","ok":false},{"t":"A taint on a node repels pods; only pods with a matching toleration can be scheduled there","ok":true},{"t":"Network rules","ok":false},{"t":"Ways to tag images","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Used to dedicate nodes (GPU nodes, for example) or keep workloads off the control plane.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m73--type-html-html-what-are-taint"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which object gives pods a stable name and IP?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Deployment","ok":false},{"t":"Service","ok":true},{"t":"ConfigMap","ok":false},{"t":"Node","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Pod IPs change. A Service's ClusterIP and DNS name don't.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m74--type-html-html-which-object-g"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is the main difference between Linux and UNIX?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Linux has no command line","ok":false},{"t":"Linux is a free, open-source, UNIX-like system developed by a community; most UNIX versions are commercial and tied to vendor hardware","ok":true},{"t":"UNIX is newer than Linux","ok":false},{"t":"Linux can only run on servers","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Linux (1991, Linus Torvalds) works like UNIX but is free and runs on almost anything. UNIX (Bell Labs, late 1960s) mostly lives on as commercial systems such as AIX and Solaris.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m75--type-html-html-what-is-the-ma"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is the Linux <strong>kernel</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The desktop environment","ok":false},{"t":"The package manager","ok":false},{"t":"The core of the OS that manages CPU, memory, devices and processes, sitting between programs and hardware","ok":true},{"t":"The first user that logs in","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Programs ask the kernel for resources through <strong>system calls</strong>. Everything else (shell, tools, desktop) runs on top.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m76--type-html-html-what-is-the-li"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which information is <strong>not</strong> stored in a file's inode?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Permissions","ok":false},{"t":"Owner","ok":false},{"t":"The file name","ok":true},{"t":"Location of the data blocks","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Names live in <strong>directory entries</strong> that point to inode numbers. That's how one inode can have several hard-link names.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m77--type-html-html-which-informat"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is a <strong>zombie</strong> process?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A process using 100% CPU","ok":false},{"t":"A finished process whose parent hasn't yet collected its exit status","ok":true},{"t":"A process running as root","ok":false},{"t":"A process with no terminal","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>It holds only a process-table entry (a PID). Fix or restart the <strong>parent</strong>. You can't kill a zombie because it's already dead.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m78--type-html-html-what-is-a-zomb"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Look at the diagram. Which file still works if <code>report.txt</code> is deleted?</p>"},{"type":"diagram","code":"flowchart LR\n  A[report.txt] --> I[(inode 4211)]\n  B[backup.txt] --> I\n  C[latest.txt] -.symlink to name.-> A"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>backup.txt</code> (a hard link to the same inode)","ok":true},{"t":"<code>latest.txt</code> (the symlink)","ok":false},{"t":"Both","ok":false},{"t":"Neither","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The data survives while <strong>any hard link</strong> points to the inode. The symlink points to the <em>name</em> <code>report.txt</code>, so it breaks.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m79--type-html-html-look-at-the-di"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In Linux, what do <strong>namespaces</strong> and <strong>cgroups</strong> provide?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Encryption and compression","ok":false},{"t":"Namespaces isolate what a process can see; cgroups limit how much CPU and memory it can use","ok":true},{"t":"User accounts and passwords","ok":false},{"t":"Networking and DNS","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>These two kernel features are what make a container: an isolated view plus resource limits.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m80--type-html-html-in-linux-what-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does <code>chmod +x deploy.sh</code> do?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Deletes the file","ok":false},{"t":"Makes it readable by everyone","ok":false},{"t":"Adds execute permission so it can be run as a program","ok":true},{"t":"Changes the owner to root","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>After this you can run it with <code>./deploy.sh</code>.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m81--type-html-html-what-does-chmo"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does <code>chmod 755 script.sh</code> set?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"rwx for the owner; r-x for group and others","ok":true},{"t":"rwx for everyone","ok":false},{"t":"rw- for the owner; r-- for everyone else","ok":false},{"t":"Owner only, nothing for anyone else","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>7 = rwx, 5 = r-x. It's the standard setting for scripts and directories.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m82--type-html-html-what-does-chmo"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What do <code>chown</code> and <code>chgrp</code> change?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Permissions and passwords","ok":false},{"t":"The owning user, and the owning group","ok":true},{"t":"The file's name and folder","ok":false},{"t":"File size limits","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>chown user:group file</code> changes both at once. <code>chmod</code> changes permissions.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m83--type-html-html-what-do-chown-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command shows used and free <strong>memory</strong> in human-readable units?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>df -h</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>free -h</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>du -sh</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>top -m</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>df</code> is disk. <code>free</code> is memory. Note that Linux uses spare RAM for cache, so look at the <strong>available</strong> column.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m84--type-html-html-which-command-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is <strong>swap</strong> space?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Extra CPU cores","ok":false},{"t":"Disk space the kernel uses as overflow when RAM is full","ok":true},{"t":"A backup of /etc","ok":false},{"t":"A network share","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Swap prevents out-of-memory crashes, but it's much slower than RAM. Heavy swapping means you need more memory.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m85--type-html-html-what-is-swap-s"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How should you stop a misbehaving process <strong>first</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>kill -9 PID</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>kill PID</code> (SIGTERM), and only use <code>-9</code> if it won't stop","ok":true},{"t":"Reboot the server","ok":false},{"t":"Delete its binary","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>SIGTERM lets the program clean up. SIGKILL (-9) can't be caught and may leave corrupt or half-written files.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m86--type-html-html-how-should-you"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command shows listening TCP ports and the processes that own them?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>ping -a</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>ss -tlnp</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>ls /ports</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>ps -ports</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>ss</code> replaced <code>netstat</code>. <code>-tlnp</code> means TCP, listening, numeric, with the process.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m87--type-html-html-which-command-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You need the PID of the running <code>nginx</code> process. Which command is quickest?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>whoami nginx</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>pgrep nginx</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>kill -0 nginx</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>ls /proc/nginx</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>pgrep</code> searches by name. <code>ps aux | grep nginx</code> also works.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m88--type-html-html-you-need-the-p"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does this crontab line do?</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"*/15 * * * * /usr/local/bin/backup.sh"}],"options":[{"t":"Runs at 15:00 every day","ok":false},{"t":"Runs every 15 minutes","ok":true},{"t":"Runs on the 15th of every month","ok":false},{"t":"Runs 15 times then stops","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>*/15</code> in the <strong>minute</strong> field means every 15 minutes. The other fields <code>* * * *</code> mean every hour, day, month and weekday.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m89--type-html-html-what-does-this"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is inside <code>/proc</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Installed programs","ok":false},{"t":"A virtual file system the kernel creates, with live information about processes and the system","ok":true},{"t":"User passwords","ok":false},{"t":"Log files","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>e.g. <code>/proc/cpuinfo</code>, <code>/proc/meminfo</code> and <code>/proc/&lt;PID&gt;/</code> for each process. Nothing there is stored on disk.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m90--type-html-html-what-is-inside"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What are <strong>system calls</strong> (syscalls)?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Phone calls to the help desk","ok":false},{"t":"The interface programs use to ask the kernel to do privileged work, such as open a file or create a process","ok":true},{"t":"Commands typed in the shell","ok":false},{"t":"Cron jobs","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>open()</code>, <code>read()</code>, <code>fork()</code> and <code>execve()</code> are syscalls. <code>strace</code> shows a program's syscalls live.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m91--type-html-html-what-are-syste"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command copies a file to another server over SSH?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>cp file user@host:/tmp/</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>scp file user@host:/tmp/</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>mv file host</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>ssh-copy file host</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>scp</code> (or the more efficient <code>rsync -avz</code>, which also handles directories and resumes).</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m92--type-html-html-which-command-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Someone ran <code>chmod 644 /bin/chmod</code>. Now <code>chmod</code> can't run. How can you fix it?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Reboot","ok":false},{"t":"Run it through the dynamic loader or another tool, e.g. <code>/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 /bin/chmod +x /bin/chmod</code> (or use <code>install</code>, <code>setfacl</code>, or Python's <code>os.chmod</code>)","ok":true},{"t":"Delete <code>/bin/chmod</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>sudo chmod +x /bin/chmod</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The binary lost its execute bit, so you need <strong>another program</strong> that can set permissions, such as the dynamic loader running chmod directly. <code>sudo</code> doesn't help because chmod itself can't run.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m93--type-html-html-someone-ran-ch"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A colleague says \"ping fails, so the server is down\". Why might that be wrong?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Ping always works","ok":false},{"t":"Ping (ICMP) is often blocked by firewalls or security groups even when the service works, so test the real port with <code>nc</code> or <code>curl</code>","ok":true},{"t":"Ping only works on Windows","ok":false},{"t":"Ping uses port 80","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A good habit from the guide: test the actual service (<code>nc -zv host 443</code>, <code>curl -v</code>), not just ICMP.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m94--type-html-html-a-colleague-sa"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command finds all <code>.yaml</code> files under the current folder and shows their permissions?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>ls *.yaml -R</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>find . -type f -name \"*.yaml\" -exec ls -l {} \\;</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>grep -r yaml .</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>cat *.yaml</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>find</code> walks the tree. <code>-exec … {} \\;</code> runs <code>ls -l</code> on each match.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m95--type-html-html-which-command-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What exit code means \"command not found\"?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"0","ok":false},{"t":"1","ok":false},{"t":"127","ok":true},{"t":"137","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>0 = success · 1 = general error · <strong>127 = not found</strong> · 137 = killed (SIGKILL, often OOM) · 143 = SIGTERM.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Revision notes","level":"Beginner","id":"m96--type-html-html-what-exit-code"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is the difference between HTTP and HTTPS?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"HTTPS is faster because it skips headers","ok":false},{"t":"HTTPS is HTTP inside TLS: encrypted, tamper-proof, and the server proves its identity with a certificate","ok":true},{"t":"HTTP is for APIs, HTTPS for websites","ok":false},{"t":"HTTPS uses UDP","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Default ports: HTTP <strong>80</strong>, HTTPS <strong>443</strong>.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m97--type-html-html-what-is-the-di"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which part of this HTTP request is the <strong>method</strong>?</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"POST /api/v1/memos HTTP/1.1\nHost: memos.example.com\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"content\": \"buy milk\"}"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>POST</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>/api/v1/memos</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>Host: memos.example.com</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>{\"content\": \"buy milk\"}</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Request line = <strong>method + path + version</strong>. Then come the headers, a blank line and the body.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m98--type-html-html-which-part-of-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which HTTP method is meant for <strong>reading</strong> data without changing anything?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"GET","ok":true},{"t":"POST","ok":false},{"t":"PUT","ok":false},{"t":"DELETE","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>GET should be safe and idempotent. POST creates, PUT/PATCH update, DELETE removes.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m99--type-html-html-which-http-met"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <code>5xx</code> status code means…</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"the client sent a bad request","ok":false},{"t":"the server failed to handle a valid request","ok":true},{"t":"the request was redirected","ok":false},{"t":"success","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>2xx success · 3xx redirect · 4xx client error · 5xx server error.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m100--type-html-html-a-5xx-status-c"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>When would you choose <strong>UDP</strong> over TCP?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"When every byte must arrive in order","ok":false},{"t":"When speed and low latency matter more than guaranteed delivery, e.g. DNS lookups, video calls, games","ok":true},{"t":"For SSH sessions","ok":false},{"t":"For database connections","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>UDP has no handshake and no retransmits. A late video frame is useless anyway.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m101--type-html-html-when-would-you"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Put the TCP three-way handshake in order.</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"ACK → SYN → SYN-ACK","ok":false},{"t":"SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK","ok":true},{"t":"SYN → ACK → FIN","ok":false},{"t":"HELLO → CERT → DONE","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Client SYN, server SYN-ACK, client ACK, and the connection is open.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m102--type-html-html-put-the-tcp-th"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does DNS do?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Encrypts traffic","ok":false},{"t":"Translates names such as memos.example.com into IP addresses","ok":true},{"t":"Assigns IP addresses to devices","ok":false},{"t":"Balances load between servers","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Handing out IP addresses is <strong>DHCP</strong>'s job, not DNS's.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m103--type-html-html-what-does-dns-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is <strong>DHCP</strong> used for?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Automatically giving devices an IP address, gateway and DNS servers when they join a network","ok":true},{"t":"Resolving domain names","ok":false},{"t":"Encrypting Wi-Fi","ok":false},{"t":"Routing between VPCs","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>That's why your laptop \"just works\" on new Wi-Fi.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m104--type-html-html-what-is-dhcp-u"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How many addresses does <code>10.0.0.0/16</code> contain?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"256","ok":false},{"t":"4,096","ok":false},{"t":"65,536","ok":true},{"t":"16","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>2^(32−16) = 65,536.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m105--type-html-html-how-many-addre"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which of these subnets does <strong>not</strong> fit inside the VPC <code>10.0.0.0/16</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>10.0.3.0/24</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>10.0.255.0/24</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>10.1.0.0/24</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>10.0.128.0/20</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A /16 fixes the first two numbers (<code>10.0</code>). <code>10.1.x.x</code> is outside the range.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m106--type-html-html-which-of-these"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is <strong>ingress</strong> traffic?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Traffic coming <strong>into</strong> a network or resource","ok":true},{"t":"Traffic going out","ok":false},{"t":"Traffic between two AZs","ok":false},{"t":"Encrypted traffic only","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Egress = outgoing. In Memos, users → ALB is ingress, and tasks → NAT → ECR is egress.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m107--type-html-html-what-is-ingres"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A <strong>switch</strong> vs a <strong>router</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"They're the same device","ok":false},{"t":"A switch connects devices within one network using MAC addresses (L2); a router moves traffic between networks using IPs (L3)","ok":true},{"t":"A router is wireless, a switch is wired","ok":false},{"t":"A switch connects to the internet, a router doesn't","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A hub (old) blindly copies traffic to every port. A switch learns which MAC is on which port.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m108--type-html-html-a-switch-vs-a-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the difference between a <strong>stateful</strong> and a <strong>stateless</strong> firewall?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Stateful firewalls are hardware, stateless are software","ok":false},{"t":"Stateful tracks connections and automatically allows replies; stateless checks every packet on its own, so you must allow return traffic yourself","ok":true},{"t":"Stateless firewalls are more secure","ok":false},{"t":"There's no difference","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>AWS <strong>security groups</strong> are stateful. <strong>NACLs</strong> are stateless.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m109--type-html-html-what-s-the-dif"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How does <strong>mTLS</strong> differ from normal TLS?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It's faster","ok":false},{"t":"Both client and server present certificates and authenticate each other","ok":true},{"t":"It skips encryption","ok":false},{"t":"It only works over UDP","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Normal TLS only proves the server's identity. mTLS is common for service-to-service and API security.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m110--type-html-html-how-does-mtls-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why would you use <strong>WebSockets</strong> instead of plain HTTPS requests?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"They're more secure","ok":false},{"t":"To keep a two-way connection open so the server can push updates instantly (chat, live dashboards)","ok":true},{"t":"To download large files","ok":false},{"t":"They don't need a port","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>HTTP is request/response. WebSockets upgrade one connection into a long-lived, two-way channel.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m111--type-html-html-why-would-you-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You can't reach a website. In which order should you investigate?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Restart the server, then check DNS","ok":false},{"t":"DNS resolves? → route reachable? → port open? → app responds correctly?","ok":true},{"t":"Check the app logs only","ok":false},{"t":"Clear browser cookies first","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Work outward from the basics, one layer at a time: <code>dig</code> → <code>traceroute</code> → <code>nc</code>/<code>curl -v</code> → logs.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m112--type-html-html-you-can-t-reac"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>curl</code> to a server immediately returns <strong>\"Connection refused\"</strong>. What does that tell you?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A firewall dropped the packets","ok":false},{"t":"The host was reached but nothing is listening on that port","ok":true},{"t":"DNS failed","ok":false},{"t":"The certificate expired","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Refused = there's an answer, but no listener. A <strong>timeout</strong> points to something dropping traffic.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m113--type-html-html-curl-to-a-serv"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Data transfer between two hosts is very slow. Which tool measures the actual throughput between them?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>dig</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>whoami</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>iperf3</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>chmod</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>iperf3</code> measures bandwidth. <code>mtr</code>/<code>traceroute</code> show latency and loss per hop. Also check the MTU, duplex settings and errors on the interfaces.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m114--type-html-html-data-transfer-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which OSI layer do IP addresses and routers belong to?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Layer 2","ok":false},{"t":"Layer 3","ok":true},{"t":"Layer 4","ok":false},{"t":"Layer 7","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>L2 is MAC/switches, <strong>L3 is IP/routers</strong>, L4 is TCP/UDP ports and L7 is HTTP/DNS.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m115--type-html-html-which-osi-laye"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which DNS record type points a name to <strong>another name</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A","ok":false},{"t":"MX","ok":false},{"t":"CNAME","ok":true},{"t":"TXT","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A → IPv4, AAAA → IPv6, CNAME → another name, MX → mail servers, TXT → text (verification).</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Revision notes","level":"Beginner","id":"m116--type-html-html-which-dns-reco"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is Infrastructure as Code?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Writing apps that run on servers","ok":false},{"t":"Defining infrastructure in version-controlled files so it can be reviewed, repeated and automated","ok":true},{"t":"A cloud provider","ok":false},{"t":"Documentation of servers","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Terraform, CloudFormation, CDK and Pulumi are all IaC tools.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m117--type-html-html-what-is-infras"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Terraform vs CloudFormation?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Terraform only works on Azure","ok":false},{"t":"Terraform works with many providers (AWS, Azure, GitHub…) and keeps its own state; CloudFormation is AWS-only and AWS manages the state","ok":true},{"t":"CloudFormation is declarative, Terraform isn't","ok":false},{"t":"They're identical","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Azure's equivalent of CloudFormation is ARM/Bicep.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m118--type-html-html-terraform-vs-c"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Terraform vs Ansible?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Terraform <strong>provisions</strong> infrastructure (declarative, with state); Ansible mostly <strong>configures</strong> servers (procedural tasks, agentless over SSH)","ok":true},{"t":"Ansible can't talk to clouds","ok":false},{"t":"Terraform configures packages inside servers","ok":false},{"t":"Ansible needs a state file","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>They're often used together: Terraform builds the servers, Ansible sets them up.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m119--type-html-html-terraform-vs-a"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is Terraform <strong>state</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The current Git branch","ok":false},{"t":"A file mapping resources in your code to real resource IDs, used to work out what to change","ok":true},{"t":"A log of every apply","ok":false},{"t":"The provider's cache","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>It can hold secrets, so keep it remote, encrypted and private, and <strong>never</strong> commit it.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m120--type-html-html-what-is-terraf"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Local vs remote state?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Local is better for teams","ok":false},{"t":"Local state is a file on your machine (fine for solo experiments); remote state (e.g. S3) is shared, locked and backed up, so it's needed for teams and CI","ok":true},{"t":"Remote state can't be encrypted","ok":false},{"t":"There's no difference","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Memos stores its state in S3 with <code>use_lockfile = true</code>.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m121--type-html-html-local-vs-remot"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is a <strong>provider</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A plugin that lets Terraform talk to a platform's API (aws, azurerm, github, kubernetes…)","ok":true},{"t":"The person who runs apply","ok":false},{"t":"A type of module","ok":false},{"t":"The state backend","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Pin provider versions (<code>~&gt; 5.0</code>) and commit <code>.terraform.lock.hcl</code>.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m122--type-html-html-what-is-a-prov"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command checks your configuration for syntax and reference errors?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>terraform fmt</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>terraform validate</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>terraform plan -check</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>terraform lint</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>fmt</code> formats HCL (<code>-check</code> fails if anything needs formatting). <code>validate</code> checks correctness.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m123--type-html-html-which-command-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How do you format all Terraform files, including in subfolders?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>terraform fmt -recursive</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>terraform validate -all</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>terraform format .</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>terraform fix</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Memos' pipeline runs <code>terraform fmt -check -recursive</code> and fails on bad formatting.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m124--type-html-html-how-do-you-for"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How does state <strong>locking</strong> work with an S3 backend today?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It doesn't: two applies can run safely","ok":false},{"t":"With <code>use_lockfile = true</code>, Terraform writes a <code>.tflock</code> object; other runs wait or fail until it's released (DynamoDB is no longer required)","ok":true},{"t":"Git branches act as locks","ok":false},{"t":"The provider locks AWS resources","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>S3 native locking arrived in Terraform 1.10. Memos uses it.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m125--type-html-html-how-does-state"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Three engineers will work on the same AWS infrastructure. What should you set up first?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Each keeps their own local state","ok":false},{"t":"A remote backend with locking, plus a PR workflow that runs <code>plan</code> in CI","ok":true},{"t":"A shared laptop","ok":false},{"t":"Email each other before applying","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Shared, locked state plus reviewed plans stops people overwriting each other's work.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m126--type-html-html-three-engineer"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How do you bring a resource created by hand under Terraform management?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Delete it and recreate it","ok":false},{"t":"Write the resource block, then use an <code>import</code> block (or <code>terraform import</code>) so state records the existing ID","ok":true},{"t":"<code>terraform refresh</code>","ok":false},{"t":"Copy its ID into a variable","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Memos used import blocks for ACM validation records that already existed.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m127--type-html-html-how-do-you-bri"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How do you pass a value from one module to another?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Global variables","ok":false},{"t":"Declare an <code>output</code> in the first module and pass <code>module.first.output_name</code> into the second module's variable","ok":true},{"t":"Read the other module's state file","ok":false},{"t":"You can't","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>e.g. <code>certificate_arn = module.acm.certificate_arn</code> in Memos' root module.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m128--type-html-html-how-do-you-pas"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command reconciles state with real infrastructure without proposing resource changes?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>terraform refresh</code> (recommended)","ok":false},{"t":"<code>terraform plan -refresh-only</code> / <code>apply -refresh-only</code> (<code>terraform refresh</code> is deprecated)","ok":true},{"t":"<code>terraform validate</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>terraform state rm</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The guide corrects the repo here: use the <code>-refresh-only</code> mode.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m129--type-html-html-which-command-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How should you handle secrets in Terraform?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Hard-code them in <code>terraform.tfvars</code> and commit it","ok":false},{"t":"Keep them out of Git; read them from Secrets Manager/SSM or environment variables, mark variables <code>sensitive</code>, and protect the state (which can still contain them)","ok":true},{"t":"Put them in outputs so they're easy to find","ok":false},{"t":"Base64-encode them in the code","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Sensitive values can still end up in <strong>state</strong>, so encrypt it and restrict access.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m130--type-html-html-how-should-you"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is <strong>Terragrunt</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A Terraform competitor from AWS","ok":false},{"t":"A thin wrapper that keeps Terraform configurations DRY across many environments and modules (shared backend config, dependencies)","ok":true},{"t":"A security scanner","ok":false},{"t":"A Terraform cloud service","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Useful at scale; not needed for a single stack like Memos.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m131--type-html-html-what-is-terrag"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What do <strong>Checkov</strong> and <strong>tfsec</strong> do?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Format Terraform","ok":false},{"t":"Static-analyse IaC for security and compliance problems (public buckets, open SGs, missing encryption)","ok":true},{"t":"Run Terraform faster","ok":false},{"t":"Store state","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Memos runs tfsec on every pipeline and documents accepted findings with <code>tfsec:ignore</code> and a reason.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Beginner","id":"m132--type-html-html-what-do-checko"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In a pipeline, where should Terraform state live?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"On the CI runner's disk","ok":false},{"t":"In the Git repo","ok":false},{"t":"In a remote backend (e.g. an encrypted, versioned S3 bucket with locking) the pipeline reads through least-privilege credentials","ok":true},{"t":"In the Docker image","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Runners are throwaway VMs. State saved there would be lost after every job.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"tech-vault","level":"Intermediate","id":"m133--type-html-html-in-a-pipeline-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does <code>-/+</code> mean in a plan?</p>"},{"type":"output","text":"-/+ resource \"aws_lb\" \"this\" {\n      ~ name = \"memos-alb\" -> \"memos-prod-alb\" # forces replacement\n    }"}],"options":[{"t":"Update in place","ok":false},{"t":"The resource will be destroyed and recreated (replaced)","ok":true},{"t":"Nothing changes","ok":false},{"t":"It's imported","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Some attributes can't be changed in place. Renaming an ALB replaces it, which means a new DNS name and a brief outage.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"Revision notes","level":"Intermediate","id":"m134--type-html-html-what-does-mean"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why prefer <code>for_each</code> over <code>count</code> for things you may remove later?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"count is deprecated","ok":false},{"t":"count tracks items by position, so removing one shifts the rest and can recreate them; for_each uses stable keys","ok":true},{"t":"for_each is faster","ok":false},{"t":"count can't create subnets","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Memos uses <code>for_each</code> over ACM's validation options.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"Revision notes","level":"Intermediate","id":"m135--type-html-html-why-prefer-for"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why does Memos keep the state bucket and OIDC role in a separate <code>bootstrap/</code> stack?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Terraform requires it","ok":false},{"t":"They must exist before the main stack (the state has to live somewhere), and they're long-lived, so destroying <code>infra/</code> won't touch them","ok":true},{"t":"To save money","ok":false},{"t":"Because bootstrap uses CloudFormation","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Separate lifecycles: long-lived foundations versus the app stack you rebuild.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"Revision notes","level":"Beginner","id":"m136--type-html-html-why-does-memos"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is the main goal of DevOps?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"To replace operations engineers with developers","ok":false},{"t":"To get small changes to users quickly and safely, with shared ownership","ok":true},{"t":"To use as many tools as possible","ok":false},{"t":"To release software less often so it is more stable","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>DevOps is culture and automation that make <strong>frequent, low-risk</strong> releases possible. Releasing <em>less</em> often usually makes each release <em>riskier</em>.</p>","topic":"DevOps","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m137--type-html-html-what-is-the-ma"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which of these is NOT usually part of a DevOps engineer's job?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Writing Terraform for cloud infrastructure","ok":false},{"t":"Building CI/CD pipelines","ok":false},{"t":"Designing the company logo","ok":true},{"t":"Setting up monitoring and alerts","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Branding is design work. Infrastructure as code, pipelines and monitoring are core DevOps tasks.</p>","topic":"DevOps","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m138--type-html-html-which-of-these"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In the Memos architecture, where does the app container actually run?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"In a public subnet with a public IP","ok":false},{"t":"On ECS Fargate in private subnets, reached only through the load balancer","ok":true},{"t":"Directly on the Application Load Balancer","ok":false},{"t":"In the ECR image registry","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The task sits in <strong>private subnets</strong> with no public IP. Users reach it only through the ALB in the public subnets. ECR just stores the image.</p>","topic":"DevOps","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m139--type-html-html-in-the-memos-a"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which part of a computer loses its contents when the power goes off?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The disk","ok":false},{"t":"RAM","ok":true},{"t":"The network card","ok":false},{"t":"An SSD","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>RAM is <strong>volatile</strong>: it's fast but temporary. Disks (HDD or SSD) keep data without power.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m140--type-html-html-which-part-of-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A request reaches the right server but the wrong program answers. What was most likely wrong?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The IP address","ok":false},{"t":"The port number","ok":true},{"t":"The MAC address","ok":false},{"t":"The DNS TTL","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The IP picks the <strong>machine</strong>. The <strong>port</strong> picks the program on it. If the port is wrong, the right server is reached but the wrong program, or no program, answers.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m141--type-html-html-a-request-reac"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A user gets <strong>403 Forbidden</strong>. What does that tell you?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The server crashed","ok":false},{"t":"The page doesn't exist","ok":false},{"t":"The server understood the request but refuses to allow it","ok":true},{"t":"The DNS lookup failed","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>403 is a <strong>4xx client</strong> error: the request reached the server, which decided you aren't allowed. A crash would be 5xx, a missing page 404, and a DNS failure never reaches the server at all.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m142--type-html-html-a-user-gets-40"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does the ALB in Memos send back when someone visits <code>http://memos.example.com</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"200 OK with the page","ok":false},{"t":"301 redirect to the https:// address","ok":true},{"t":"404 Not Found","ok":false},{"t":"502 Bad Gateway","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The HTTP listener on port 80 has a single redirect rule: send <strong>301</strong> to port 443 over HTTPS.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m143--type-html-html-what-does-the-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Terraform, the AWS CLI and the AWS web console all create resources by…</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"editing the same config file","ok":false},{"t":"calling the same AWS APIs","ok":true},{"t":"logging in to the servers over SSH","ok":false},{"t":"running CloudFormation behind the scenes","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>They are all clients of the <strong>AWS APIs</strong>. That's why anything you can click in the console can also be automated in code.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m144--type-html-html-terraform-the-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command shows the last lines of a log file and keeps printing new lines as they arrive?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>cat app.log</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>head -f app.log</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>tail -f app.log</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>less app.log</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>tail -f</code> <strong>follows</strong> the file. It's the go-to for watching logs live.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m145--type-html-html-which-command-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A server says \"No space left on device\", but <code>df -h</code> shows 40% free. What should you check next?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"RAM usage with <code>free -h</code>","ok":false},{"t":"Inode usage with <code>df -i</code>","ok":true},{"t":"The CPU load with <code>top</code>","ok":false},{"t":"Open ports with <code>ss -tlnp</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Every file needs an inode. Millions of tiny files can use them all up while disk space remains, and <code>df -i</code> shows it.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m146--type-html-html-a-server-says-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does <code>chmod 640 secrets.env</code> give?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Owner read/write/execute, group read, others nothing","ok":false},{"t":"Owner read/write, group read, others nothing","ok":true},{"t":"Everyone can read","ok":false},{"t":"Owner read only","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>6 = 4+2 (rw), 4 = r, 0 = nothing. So: owner <strong>rw-</strong>, group <strong>r--</strong>, others <strong>---</strong>.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m147--type-html-html-what-does-chmo"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You can <code>ls</code> a folder but <code>cd</code> into it fails with \"Permission denied\". Which permission is missing?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"r","ok":false},{"t":"w","ok":false},{"t":"x","ok":true},{"t":"s","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>On directories, <strong>x</strong> means \"enter or traverse\". With <code>r</code> but no <code>x</code> you can see the names but can't go in.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m148--type-html-html-you-can-ls-a-f"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A container stops with <strong>exit code 137</strong>. What is the most likely cause?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The command wasn't found","ok":false},{"t":"It finished successfully","ok":false},{"t":"It was killed with SIGKILL, often for using too much memory","ok":true},{"t":"It was stopped politely with SIGTERM","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>137 = 128 + 9 (SIGKILL). On ECS or Kubernetes that's usually the <strong>out-of-memory</strong> killer. Memos' task has only 512 MB.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m149--type-html-html-a-container-st"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command installs a package in an Alpine-based container without keeping the package index (so the image stays small)?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>apt install curl</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>yum install curl</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>apk add --no-cache curl</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>brew install curl</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Alpine uses <strong>apk</strong>. <code>--no-cache</code> skips storing the index, which keeps image layers small.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m150--type-html-html-which-command-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does this print? <code>name=memos; echo '$name is ${name}'</code></p>"}],"options":[{"t":"memos is memos","ok":false},{"t":"$name is ${name}","ok":true},{"t":"memos is ${name}","ok":false},{"t":"an error","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><strong>Single quotes</strong> stop all expansion, so the text is printed exactly as typed.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m151--type-html-html-what-does-this"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command sends both normal output and errors into <code>run.log</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>./run.sh 2&gt; run.log</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>./run.sh &gt; run.log 2&gt;&amp;1</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>./run.sh | run.log</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>./run.sh &gt;&gt; 2 run.log</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>&gt; run.log</code> sends stdout to the file, then <code>2&gt;&amp;1</code> sends stderr to wherever stdout is going. Order matters.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m152--type-html-html-which-command-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In <code>make build &amp;&amp; make deploy</code>, when does <code>make deploy</code> run?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Only if <code>make build</code> exits with code 0","ok":true},{"t":"Only if <code>make build</code> fails","ok":false},{"t":"Always, after <code>make build</code>","ok":false},{"t":"At the same time as <code>make build</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>&amp;&amp;</code> means \"and then, if that worked\". <code>||</code> means \"otherwise\".</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m153--type-html-html-in-make-build-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why add <code>set -o pipefail</code> to a script?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"To make pipes run faster","ok":false},{"t":"So a failure anywhere in a pipeline makes the whole pipeline fail","ok":true},{"t":"To stop the script printing errors","ok":false},{"t":"So variables don't need quotes","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Without it, <code>failing_cmd | grep x</code> reports grep's exit code and hides the failure.</p>","topic":"Linux","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m154--type-html-html-why-add-set-o-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An Application Load Balancer can route <code>/api/*</code> to one service and <code>/</code> to another. At which layer is it working?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Layer 2","ok":false},{"t":"Layer 3","ok":false},{"t":"Layer 4","ok":false},{"t":"Layer 7","ok":true}],"explain":"<p>Paths and hostnames are <strong>HTTP</strong> details, which is layer 7. A layer-4 balancer only sees IPs and ports.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m155--type-html-html-an-application"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>How many usable IP addresses does a <code>/24</code> subnet have in AWS?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"256","ok":false},{"t":"254","ok":false},{"t":"251","ok":true},{"t":"250","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>256 total minus <strong>5 reserved by AWS</strong>. (On an ordinary network you'd lose 2, giving 254.)</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m156--type-html-html-how-many-usabl"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which CIDR block contains <strong>more</strong> addresses?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>10.0.0.0/20</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>10.0.0.0/24</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>10.0.0.0/26</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>10.0.0.0/28</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A <strong>smaller</strong> prefix means <strong>more</strong> addresses: /20 = 4,096, /24 = 256, /26 = 64, /28 = 16.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m157--type-html-html-which-cidr-blo"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><code>curl http://10.0.3.25:8081</code> hangs and then times out. What's the most likely cause?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The app crashed","ok":false},{"t":"Nothing is listening on port 8081","ok":false},{"t":"A firewall, security group or route is dropping the traffic","ok":true},{"t":"The DNS name is wrong","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A <strong>timeout</strong> means the packets never got a reply. \"Nothing listening\" would give <strong>connection refused</strong> straight away.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m158--type-html-html-curl-http-10-0"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why does Memos use a Route 53 <strong>alias A record</strong> for the load balancer instead of an ordinary A record?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"An ALB's IP addresses change, and an alias follows the ALB automatically","ok":true},{"t":"A records can't point to AWS","ok":false},{"t":"Alias records are faster to type","ok":false},{"t":"It's required for HTTPS","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>ALBs don't have fixed IPs. An <strong>alias</strong> record targets the ALB itself and always returns its current addresses. It's also free and works at the zone apex.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m159--type-html-html-why-does-memos"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Where is TLS terminated in the Memos architecture?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Inside the Memos container","ok":false},{"t":"At the Application Load Balancer","ok":true},{"t":"At Route 53","ok":false},{"t":"At the NAT gateway","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The ALB holds the ACM certificate and decrypts. Behind it, traffic travels inside the VPC to port 8081.</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m160--type-html-html-where-is-tls-t"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command shows which programs are listening on which TCP ports on this machine?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>ping localhost</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>dig localhost</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>ss -tlnp</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>traceroute localhost</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>ss -tlnp</code> = <strong>t</strong>cp, <strong>l</strong>istening, <strong>n</strong>umeric, with the <strong>p</strong>rocess. It's the quick way to spot \"listening on 5230, not 8081\".</p>","topic":"Networking","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m161--type-html-html-which-command-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You edited a file and ran <code>git commit</code>, but the change isn't in the commit. What did you forget?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>git push</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>git add</code> (staging the file)","ok":true},{"t":"<code>git pull</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>git init</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Commits only include what's <strong>staged</strong>. Run <code>git add &lt;file&gt;</code> first, or <code>git commit -a</code> for files Git already tracks.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m162--type-html-html-you-edited-a-f"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>When is <code>git rebase</code> a bad idea?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"On your local feature branch before opening a PR","ok":false},{"t":"On a branch other people have already pulled","ok":true},{"t":"To update your branch with the latest main","ok":false},{"t":"To tidy up commits before review","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Rebasing <strong>rewrites</strong> commits. If others built on the old ones, their history no longer matches yours.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m163--type-html-html-when-is-git-re"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A bad commit was pushed to <code>main</code> an hour ago and teammates have pulled it. What's the safe way to undo it?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>git reset --hard HEAD~1</code> then <code>git push --force</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>git revert &lt;sha&gt;</code> then push","ok":true},{"t":"Delete the repository and re-create it","ok":false},{"t":"<code>git stash</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>revert</code> creates a <strong>new</strong> commit that reverses the bad one. History stays intact for everyone.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m164--type-html-html-a-bad-commit-w"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An AWS access key was pushed to a public repo. What's the <strong>first</strong> thing to do?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Delete the file and push again","ok":false},{"t":"Make the repo private","ok":false},{"t":"Deactivate and rotate the key in AWS immediately","ok":true},{"t":"Rewrite the Git history","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Assume it's already stolen. <strong>Revoke it first</strong>, then clean up history. Deleting the file leaves the key in history.</p>","topic":"Git","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m165--type-html-html-an-aws-access-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What do containers share that virtual machines don't?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The application code","ok":false},{"t":"The host operating system's kernel","ok":true},{"t":"Their IP addresses","ok":false},{"t":"Their file system","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Containers share the host <strong>kernel</strong>. VMs each boot their own OS on a hypervisor. That's why containers are lighter but isolate a little less strongly.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m166--type-html-html-what-do-contai"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which instruction runs when the container <strong>starts</strong>, rather than when the image is built?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>RUN</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>COPY</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>ENTRYPOINT</code> / <code>CMD</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>FROM</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>RUN</code> happens during <code>docker build</code>. <code>ENTRYPOINT</code>/<code>CMD</code> define the process that runs each time a container starts.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m167--type-html-html-which-instruct"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In a multi-stage build, what ends up in the final image?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Every stage, stacked together","ok":false},{"t":"Only the last stage, plus anything it copies from earlier stages","ok":true},{"t":"Only the first stage","ok":false},{"t":"The build cache","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Earlier stages are <strong>build-only</strong>. <code>COPY --from=&lt;stage&gt;</code> pulls just the artefacts you need into the final stage.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m168--type-html-html-in-a-multi-sta"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You run <code>docker run -p 9000:8081 memos:dev</code>. Which URL reaches the app from your laptop?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>http://localhost:8081</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>http://localhost:9000</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>http://localhost:5230</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>http://memos:8081</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>-p HOST:CONTAINER</code>, so host port <strong>9000</strong> forwards to the container's 8081.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m169--type-html-html-you-run-docker"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why is baking an API key into a Docker image a bad idea, even if you delete it in a later layer?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It makes the image too large","ok":false},{"t":"Earlier layers still contain it, and anyone who can pull the image can extract it","ok":true},{"t":"Docker refuses to build it","ok":false},{"t":"It slows the container down","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Layers are <strong>additive</strong>. A file deleted later still exists in the earlier layer. Inject secrets at runtime instead.</p>","topic":"Docker","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m170--type-html-html-why-is-baking-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>On <strong>EC2</strong>, who is responsible for patching the operating system?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"AWS","ok":false},{"t":"You (the customer)","ok":true},{"t":"Nobody, because it patches itself","ok":false},{"t":"The hypervisor","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>EC2 is IaaS, so the OS is <strong>yours</strong> to patch. On Fargate, AWS patches the hosts. That's the shared responsibility model.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m171--type-html-html-on-ec2-who-is-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A company must keep customer data inside the UK. Which factor decides their Region first?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Price","ok":false},{"t":"Latency","ok":false},{"t":"Compliance (data residency)","ok":true},{"t":"Number of AZs","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Legal requirements come <strong>before</strong> speed or cost. Here that means eu-west-2 (London).</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m172--type-html-html-a-company-must"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which of these keeps costing money even when nobody is using your app?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A Lambda function with no invocations","ok":false},{"t":"An empty S3 bucket","ok":false},{"t":"A NAT gateway","ok":true},{"t":"An IAM role","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>NAT gateways bill <strong>per hour</strong> whether or not traffic flows, and per GB on top. Idle Lambda, empty buckets and IAM cost nothing.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m173--type-html-html-which-of-these"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A user is in a group whose policy <strong>allows</strong> <code>s3:*</code>, and also has a policy that <strong>denies</strong> <code>s3:DeleteBucket</code>. Can they delete a bucket?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Yes, because the group Allow covers it","ok":false},{"t":"No, because an explicit Deny always wins","ok":true},{"t":"Yes, but only buckets they created","ok":false},{"t":"It depends which policy was attached first","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Explicit <strong>Deny</strong> beats any Allow, every time, whatever the order.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m174--type-html-html-a-user-is-in-a"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why use an IAM <strong>role</strong> instead of an IAM user with access keys for an app or a pipeline?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Roles are cheaper","ok":false},{"t":"Roles give temporary credentials that expire, so there are no long-lived keys to leak","ok":true},{"t":"Users can't call AWS APIs","ok":false},{"t":"Roles skip permission checks","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Long-lived keys are the most common source of AWS breaches. A role's credentials come from STS and <strong>expire</strong>.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m175--type-html-html-why-use-an-iam"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which command tells you which AWS account and identity your CLI is currently using?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>aws whoami</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>aws sts get-caller-identity</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>aws iam list-users</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>aws configure list-accounts</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>sts get-caller-identity</code> is the first thing to run when \"access denied\" looks wrong. It's often the wrong account or role.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Beginner","fromLesson":true,"id":"m176--type-html-html-which-command-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A nightly batch job can safely be restarted if interrupted. Which pricing option saves the most?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"On-Demand","ok":false},{"t":"Reserved Instances","ok":false},{"t":"Spot Instances","ok":true},{"t":"Dedicated Hosts","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><strong>Spot</strong> uses spare capacity at up to ~90% off. It can be reclaimed, which is fine for work that can restart.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m177--type-html-html-a-nightly-batc"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Traffic always peaks Wednesdays and Fridays from 9am to 7pm. What's the simplest way to have capacity ready in advance?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Scale when CPU reaches 100%","ok":false},{"t":"A scheduled scaling action before 9am on those days","ok":true},{"t":"Buy the biggest instance type","ok":false},{"t":"Manually add servers each morning","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Known, repeating peaks → <strong>scheduled scaling</strong>. You can still add target tracking for anything unexpected.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m178--type-html-html-traffic-always"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why wouldn't you run the Memos web app on Lambda as it is?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Lambda can't run Go","ok":false},{"t":"Memos is a long-running server that keeps local SQLite state, which doesn't fit short, stateless functions","ok":true},{"t":"Lambda is more expensive at any scale","ok":false},{"t":"Lambda can't sit behind HTTPS","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Lambda suits short, stateless, event-driven code. Memos is a long-running HTTP server with local state, so containers fit better.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m179--type-html-html-why-wouldn-t-y"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Memos loses its SQLite data whenever the Fargate task is replaced. Which storage could fix that without changing the database?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"S3, mounted as a disk","ok":false},{"t":"EBS, shared across AZs","ok":false},{"t":"EFS, mounted into the task at /var/opt/memos","ok":true},{"t":"Instance store","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><strong>EFS</strong> is a network file system that ECS tasks can mount and that survives task replacement. (A managed database like RDS is the better long-term fix.)</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m180--type-html-html-memos-loses-it"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does RDS <strong>Multi-AZ</strong> give you?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Faster reads","ok":false},{"t":"Automatic failover to a standby in another AZ","ok":true},{"t":"A copy in another Region","ok":false},{"t":"Lower cost","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Multi-AZ is for <strong>availability</strong>. For faster reads, add <strong>read replicas</strong>.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m181--type-html-html-what-does-rds-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why does Memos create subnets in <strong>two</strong> Availability Zones?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"AWS requires at least two subnets","ok":false},{"t":"So the network (and the ALB) keeps working if one AZ fails","ok":true},{"t":"To get more IP addresses","ok":false},{"t":"Because NAT gateways need two subnets","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Spreading across AZs is the basis of <strong>high availability</strong>. ALBs also require subnets in at least two AZs.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m182--type-html-html-why-does-memos"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A private subnet's route table sends <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> to a NAT gateway. What can its instances do?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Receive connections from the internet","ok":false},{"t":"Start outbound connections to the internet, but not accept inbound ones","ok":true},{"t":"Nothing: private subnets are fully offline","ok":false},{"t":"Only talk to S3","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>NAT lets traffic <strong>out</strong> and the replies come back. New inbound connections from the internet aren't possible.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m183--type-html-html-a-private-subn"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why does the Memos ECS security group allow port 8081 <strong>from the ALB's security group</strong> rather than from <code>10.0.0.0/16</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It's required by ECS","ok":false},{"t":"It allows only the load balancer, not every other resource in the VPC","ok":true},{"t":"Security groups can't use CIDR ranges","ok":false},{"t":"It's faster","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>SG-to-SG rules are the tightest option: only traffic from resources in the ALB SG can reach the task.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m184--type-html-html-why-does-the-m"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You need to <strong>block one specific IP address</strong> from reaching a subnet. What should you use?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"A security group deny rule","ok":false},{"t":"A network ACL deny rule","ok":true},{"t":"A route table entry","ok":false},{"t":"An IAM policy","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Security groups <strong>can't deny</strong>. NACLs have deny rules (WAF is another option for HTTP traffic).</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m185--type-html-html-you-need-to-bl"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which VPC endpoint type is <strong>free</strong> and covers S3?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Gateway endpoint","ok":true},{"t":"Interface endpoint","ok":false},{"t":"NAT endpoint","ok":false},{"t":"Transit endpoint","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Gateway endpoints (S3 and DynamoDB only) are free route-table entries. Interface endpoints are network interfaces billed per hour.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m186--type-html-html-which-vpc-endp"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A game server needs fixed IP addresses and plain TCP on a custom port. Which load balancer fits?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"ALB","ok":false},{"t":"NLB","ok":true},{"t":"Classic Load Balancer","ok":false},{"t":"CloudFront","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The <strong>NLB</strong> works at layer 4 and offers static IPs per AZ. The ALB is for HTTP(S).</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m187--type-html-html-a-game-server-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Right after a deploy the ALB returns <strong>503</strong>. What's the most likely situation?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The certificate expired","ok":false},{"t":"There are no registered targets: the new task isn't running or never registered","ok":true},{"t":"DNS is pointing to the wrong place","ok":false},{"t":"The app is slow","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>503 from an ALB means there's nothing in the target group to send to. Check the ECS service events and the task's stopped reason.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m188--type-html-html-right-after-a-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why can't you use a CNAME record for <code>example.com</code> itself (the zone apex)?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"CNAMEs only work with IP addresses","ok":false},{"t":"DNS rules forbid a CNAME at the apex, where NS and SOA records must exist. Route 53 alias records solve this.","ok":true},{"t":"Route 53 doesn't support CNAMEs","ok":false},{"t":"CNAMEs can't point to AWS resources","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The apex must hold NS and SOA records, and a CNAME can't coexist with other records. <strong>Alias</strong> gets around it.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m189--type-html-html-why-can-t-you-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why does Memos use <strong>DNS</strong> validation for its ACM certificate instead of email validation?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Email validation costs money","ok":false},{"t":"It can be fully automated in Terraform, and it lets ACM renew the certificate automatically","ok":true},{"t":"Email validation doesn't work for subdomains","ok":false},{"t":"DNS validation is faster to type","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>As long as the validation CNAME stays in Route 53, ACM renews automatically, with no humans and no expiry surprises.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m190--type-html-html-why-does-memos"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why tag images with the Git commit SHA instead of <code>latest</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"SHAs are shorter","ok":false},{"t":"Each SHA is unique and maps to exact code, so you always know what's running and can roll back","ok":true},{"t":"ECR requires SHA tags","ok":false},{"t":"<code>latest</code> is slower to pull","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>latest</code> changes meaning every push. A SHA tag is a permanent, traceable version.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m191--type-html-html-why-tag-images"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does an ECS <strong>service</strong> do that a single task doesn't?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Builds the Docker image","ok":false},{"t":"Keeps the desired number of tasks running, replaces failed ones and registers them with the load balancer","ok":true},{"t":"Stores the image","ok":false},{"t":"Defines CPU and memory","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The <strong>service</strong> is the controller. The <strong>task definition</strong> is the blueprint, and a <strong>task</strong> is one running copy.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m192--type-html-html-what-does-an-e"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A new task fails with <strong>CannotPullContainerError</strong>, and you suspect permissions. Which role do you check?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The execution role","ok":true},{"t":"The task role","ok":false},{"t":"The GitHub Actions OIDC role","ok":false},{"t":"The ALB's role","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Pulling images, writing logs and fetching secrets all happen <strong>before</strong> your code runs, so they're done by ECS using the <strong>execution role</strong>.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m193--type-html-html-a-new-task-fai"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does enabling the ECS deployment circuit breaker with <code>rollback = true</code> give you?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Faster image pulls","ok":false},{"t":"Automatic return to the last working deployment if new tasks can't become healthy","ok":true},{"t":"Blue/green deployments","ok":false},{"t":"Automatic scaling","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>It detects a failing rollout and <strong>reverts by itself</strong>, with no human needed at 3 am.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m194--type-html-html-what-does-enab"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why can't you just set <code>desired_count = 3</code> on Memos today to make it highly available?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Fargate only allows one task per service","ok":false},{"t":"Each task would have its own separate SQLite database, so users would see different data","ok":true},{"t":"The ALB can only route to one task","ok":false},{"t":"It would exceed the VPC's IP addresses","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>State lives <strong>inside</strong> each task. Move the data to RDS or EFS first, then scale out.</p>","topic":"AWS","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m195--type-html-html-why-can-t-you-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What does it mean that Terraform is <strong>declarative</strong>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"You list every API call in order","ok":false},{"t":"You describe the end state and Terraform works out the steps","ok":true},{"t":"It only works on AWS","ok":false},{"t":"It runs on a schedule","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>You declare \"a VPC with this CIDR should exist\", and Terraform decides whether to create, update or leave it alone.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m196--type-html-html-what-does-it-m"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Why does Memos' pipeline run <code>terraform plan -out=tfplan</code> and then <code>terraform apply tfplan</code>?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It's faster than a plain apply","ok":false},{"t":"So exactly the reviewed plan is applied, with no surprise changes in between","ok":true},{"t":"<code>apply</code> can't run without a file","ok":false},{"t":"To skip the refresh","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A saved plan guarantees what you saw is what gets applied, even if something changed in the meantime.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m197--type-html-html-why-does-memos"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Two engineers run <code>terraform apply</code> at the same moment against the Memos backend. What happens?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Both succeed and the state merges","ok":false},{"t":"The second one fails to get the state lock and changes nothing","ok":true},{"t":"The state file is corrupted","ok":false},{"t":"The newest apply wins","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>use_lockfile = true</code> creates a lock object. The second run gets \"Error acquiring the state lock\".</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m198--type-html-html-two-engineers-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You manage subnets with <code>count</code> from a list of three CIDRs and delete the <strong>middle</strong> one. What could happen?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Only the middle subnet is destroyed","ok":false},{"t":"The third subnet's index shifts, so Terraform may destroy and recreate it","ok":true},{"t":"Nothing, because count is stable","ok":false},{"t":"Terraform refuses to plan","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>count</code> identifies resources by <strong>position</strong>. Use <code>for_each</code> with stable keys to avoid it.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m199--type-html-html-you-manage-sub"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A security group rule was added by hand in the console. How do you see this drift <strong>without</strong> changing anything?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>terraform apply</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>terraform destroy</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>terraform plan -refresh-only</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>terraform fmt</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>-refresh-only</code> compares state with reality and reports differences without proposing changes to your resources.</p>","topic":"Terraform","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m200--type-html-html-a-security-gro"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What's the difference between continuous delivery and continuous deployment?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Delivery is for apps; deployment is for infrastructure","ok":false},{"t":"Delivery keeps every change releasable but needs a human to release; deployment releases automatically","ok":true},{"t":"There is no difference","ok":false},{"t":"Deployment skips testing","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Both automate build and test. The difference is whether production needs a <strong>manual approval</strong>.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m201--type-html-html-what-s-the-dif"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In GitHub Actions, how do you make a job wait for another job to finish?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Put them in the same step","ok":false},{"t":"Use <code>needs: &lt;job-id&gt;</code>","ok":true},{"t":"Use <code>on: workflow_run</code>","ok":false},{"t":"Jobs always run in order","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Jobs run <strong>in parallel</strong> by default. <code>needs:</code> creates the dependency (<code>health-check</code> needs <code>deploy</code> in Memos).</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m202--type-html-html-in-github-acti"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>The OIDC step fails with \"Not authorized to perform sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity\". What do you check first?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The Docker image tag","ok":false},{"t":"The role's trust policy: do the <code>sub</code> and <code>aud</code> conditions match the token GitHub sent?","ok":true},{"t":"The ECS service events","ok":false},{"t":"The S3 bucket policy","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Almost every OIDC failure is a <strong>trust-policy mismatch</strong> (wrong repo name, branch, environment or audience), or a missing <code>permissions: id-token: write</code>.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m203--type-html-html-the-oidc-step-"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>You change only <code>infra/modules/alb/main.tf</code> and merge to main. Why doesn't anything deploy?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"tfsec blocked it","ok":false},{"t":"build.yml's path filter only matches app/**, Dockerfile and .dockerignore, so the build (and the deploy that follows it) never starts","ok":true},{"t":"Terraform changes need a pull request","ok":false},{"t":"The OIDC role can't change ALBs","ok":false}],"explain":"<p><code>deploy.yml</code> runs <strong>after</strong> <code>build.yml</code>. No build means no deploy, so trigger it manually or add an <code>infra/**</code> trigger.</p>","topic":"CI/CD","source":"Lesson check","level":"Intermediate","fromLesson":true,"id":"m204--type-html-html-you-change-onl"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which pillar of observability shows the path of a <strong>single request</strong> through several services?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Logs","ok":false},{"t":"Metrics","ok":false},{"t":"Traces","ok":true},{"t":"Alarms","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>A <strong>trace</strong> follows one request across hops, with timings for each, and shows where it's slow.</p>","topic":"Troubleshooting","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m205--type-html-html-which-pillar-o"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An SLO is 99.9% availability over 30 days. Roughly how much downtime does the error budget allow?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"4 minutes","ok":false},{"t":"43 minutes","ok":true},{"t":"7 hours","ok":false},{"t":"3 days","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>30 days × 24 × 60 = 43,200 minutes, and 0.1% of that ≈ <strong>43 minutes</strong>.</p>","topic":"Troubleshooting","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m206--type-html-html-an-slo-is-99-9"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Users report errors two minutes after a new deploy. What's the best <strong>first</strong> action?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Read through the code changes line by line","ok":false},{"t":"Roll back to the previous version, then investigate","ok":true},{"t":"Restart the database","ok":false},{"t":"Wait to see if it fixes itself","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Restore service first. With immutable SHA tags, rolling back is fast and safe. Debug afterwards.</p>","topic":"Troubleshooting","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m207--type-html-html-users-report-e"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A task stops with <code>CannotPullContainerError</code>. It runs in a private subnet. Which is <strong>not</strong> a likely cause?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"The private route table has no route to a NAT gateway","ok":false},{"t":"The execution role lacks ECR permissions","ok":false},{"t":"The image tag doesn't exist in ECR","ok":false},{"t":"The ALB's health check path is wrong","ok":true}],"explain":"<p>Pulling happens <strong>before</strong> the app starts, so health checks don't matter yet. Routing, IAM and the tag itself do.</p>","topic":"Troubleshooting","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m208--type-html-html-a-task-stops-w"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which AWS service can <strong>automatically rotate</strong> a database password?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"KMS","ok":false},{"t":"Secrets Manager","ok":true},{"t":"IAM","ok":false},{"t":"S3","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Secrets Manager has built-in rotation (for RDS and others). Parameter Store stores secrets but doesn't rotate them for you.</p>","topic":"Security","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m209--type-html-html-which-aws-serv"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Someone deleted a security group last night. Which service tells you who did it?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"CloudWatch Metrics","ok":false},{"t":"CloudTrail","ok":true},{"t":"GuardDuty","ok":false},{"t":"Inspector","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>CloudTrail logs every API call, including <code>DeleteSecurityGroup</code>, with the identity that made it.</p>","topic":"Security","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m210--type-html-html-someone-delete"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Memos keeps its SQLite data inside the container with no backups. What are its RPO and RTO for data if a task is replaced?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"RPO zero, RTO zero","ok":false},{"t":"The data is simply lost: there's no recovery point at all","ok":true},{"t":"RPO 24 hours, RTO 1 hour","ok":false},{"t":"RPO 7 days, because of log retention","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>With no backup and ephemeral storage, there's nothing to restore. That's why moving to RDS (with backups) or EFS is the top improvement.</p>","topic":"Security","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m211--type-html-html-memos-keeps-it"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which is a <strong>free</strong> way to stop Memos' ECR image layer downloads going through the NAT gateway?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"An interface endpoint for ECR","ok":false},{"t":"An S3 gateway endpoint (image layers are stored in S3)","ok":true},{"t":"A second NAT gateway","ok":false},{"t":"CloudFront","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Gateway endpoints for S3 cost nothing. You still need the ECR API endpoints (paid) or the NAT for the small API calls.</p>","topic":"Security","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m212--type-html-html-which-is-a-fre"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>What is the core idea behind how Kubernetes keeps applications running?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"It restarts the whole cluster every hour","ok":false},{"t":"Controllers continuously reconcile the actual state with the declared desired state","ok":true},{"t":"It runs every container twice","ok":false},{"t":"Developers restart pods by hand","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Declare the state you want, and control loops make reality match it. That's how self-healing works.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m213--type-html-html-what-is-the-co"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Which component decides <strong>which node</strong> a new pod is placed on?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"kubelet","ok":false},{"t":"etcd","ok":false},{"t":"kube-scheduler","ok":true},{"t":"kube-proxy","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The <strong>scheduler</strong> looks at resources, taints and affinity rules and picks a node. The kubelet on that node then runs the pod.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m214--type-html-html-which-componen"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A pod is running but still warming its cache, so it shouldn't get traffic yet. Which probe handles this?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Liveness probe","ok":false},{"t":"Readiness probe","ok":true},{"t":"Startup quota","ok":false},{"t":"Node affinity","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Readiness controls <strong>traffic</strong>. Liveness controls <strong>restarts</strong>. Mixing them up causes restart loops.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m215--type-html-html-a-pod-is-runni"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>A pod shows <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>. What's the most useful first command?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"<code>kubectl delete node</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code>","ok":true},{"t":"<code>kubectl get svc</code>","ok":false},{"t":"<code>kubectl scale --replicas=0</code>","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The container keeps crashing, so read the <strong>previous</strong> container's logs to see why it exited.</p>","topic":"Kubernetes","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m216--type-html-html-a-pod-shows-cr"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In a project story, which part do interviewers value most?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"How long it took","ok":false},{"t":"Which tools you used","ok":false},{"t":"What you learned and now do differently","ok":true},{"t":"Who caused the problem","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>The <strong>lesson</strong> shows judgement and growth, and stops the story being just a list of facts.</p>","topic":"Interview","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m217--type-html-html-in-a-project-s"},{"q":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>An interviewer asks about a tool you've never used. What's the best approach?</p>"}],"options":[{"t":"Pretend you've used it","ok":false},{"t":"Say \"I don't know\" and stop","ok":false},{"t":"Be honest, explain what you understand it does, and link it to similar tools you have used","ok":true},{"t":"Change the subject to Memos","ok":false}],"explain":"<p>Honesty plus a bridge to related experience shows you can learn, which is what they're really testing.</p>","topic":"Interview","source":"Lesson check","level":"Advanced","fromLesson":true,"id":"m218--type-html-html-an-interviewer"}];
